1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,707
This project studies how a wheeled service robot
can share urban pedestrian space. We compare
explicit local

2
00:00:06,707 --> 00:00:13,414
planning with vision and language guidance in
three simulated street families. The main result
is an improvement

3
00:00:13,414 --> 00:00:20,121
from geometric local selection, while the
trained visual selector does not outperform that
baseline. This is a

4
00:00:20,121 --> 00:00:26,828
rehearsal copy: our group identity, members and
agreed work percentages must be completed before
submission.

5
00:00:28,828 --> 00:00:36,959
A robot can slow down for a pedestrian, but
slowing alone cannot take it around an
obstruction.

6
00:00:36,959 --> 00:00:45,089
Our question has two parts. First, does
constrained local trajectory selection improve
contact-free completion across spatial
conditions?

7
00:00:45,089 --> 00:00:53,220
Second, does a compact vision–language model add
value beyond geometric selection? The project
combines artificial intelligence for

8
00:00:53,220 --> 00:01:01,351
action adaptation, data science for matched
evaluation, and computational media for
procedural three-dimensional simulation and
visual evidence.

9
00:01:03,351 --> 00:01:10,002
We use intersections, straight sidewalks, and
streets containing a curve. Each family has two
nominal clear-width bands,

10
00:01:10,002 --> 00:01:16,654
two pedestrian counts, and ten fresh scene
seeds. That gives one hundred and twenty
conditions per method.

11
00:01:16,654 --> 00:01:23,306
The images are native simulator captures.
Coordinates are local metres, not observations
from a real city. Five

12
00:01:23,306 --> 00:01:29,958
and fifteen describe agent counts, not crowd
density.

13
00:01:31,958 --> 00:01:40,270
This project builds on the retained single-scene
pilot. We keep its route-following and speed-
forecast comparisons, but add

14
00:01:40,270 --> 00:01:48,583
three scene families, a constrained lateral
trajectory library, an MPPI-style sampling
controller, and actual multimodal adapter
training.

15
00:01:48,583 --> 00:01:56,896
We also add training-seed checks, input
removals, curve exclusion, and controlled
blockages. These are project-level extensions.
Pure

16
00:01:56,896 --> 00:02:05,208
Pursuit, local search, predictive control, LoRA
and the simulator itself come from existing
work.

17
00:02:07,208 --> 00:02:14,296
The shared route provides a direction of travel.
The local library offers five lateral
trajectories and physical

18
00:02:14,296 --> 00:02:21,383
stopping. The geometric selector ranks feasible
options, while the adapted model proposes one
action token every second.

19
00:02:21,383 --> 00:02:28,471
At each tenth of a second, the execution layer
checks predicted footprints, pedestrians,
obstacles and walkable space.

20
00:02:28,471 --> 00:02:35,559
An infeasible model proposal falls back to
geometric selection. If no candidate is
feasible, the robot brakes.

21
00:02:35,559 --> 00:02:42,646
Native physics, rather than direct position
changes, moves the robot.

22
00:02:44,646 --> 00:02:51,749
The model is SmolVLM2 with about five hundred
million base parameters. Six labels identify the
five paths

23
00:02:51,749 --> 00:02:58,851
and stopping. We freeze the backbone and train
about one point one million low-rank parameters
in visual

24
00:02:58,851 --> 00:03:05,953
and language attention. Training uses five
hundred and ninety-eight decisions, with three
hundred and four validation decisions

25
00:03:05,953 --> 00:03:13,055
from separate scene seeds. This is supervised
imitation of geometric predictions, not
reinforcement learning or counterfactual trials.

26
00:03:13,055 --> 00:03:20,157
We train ten epochs for each seed and select
checkpoints on validation only.

27
00:03:22,157 --> 00:03:30,325
The comparisons answer different questions.
Regulated Pure Pursuit-inspired control provides
regulated path following. Speed forecasting
changes timing

28
00:03:30,325 --> 00:03:38,493
along that route. Dynamic Window Approach-style
control searches local commands. Model
Predictive Path Integral-style control samples
ninety-six

29
00:03:38,493 --> 00:03:46,660
sequences. Our geometric selector tests the new
lateral candidate hierarchy. Frozen and adapted
visual models test pretrained

30
00:03:46,660 --> 00:03:54,828
action use and task adaptation. The older
controllers do not receive every new candidate
filter, and the

31
00:03:54,828 --> 00:04:02,996
model does not receive the teacher's explicit
pedestrian velocities, so attribution must
remain bounded.

32
00:04:04,996 --> 00:04:12,332
An episode has a one hundred and twenty second
simulated budget. Strict arrival requires the
native destination

33
00:04:12,332 --> 00:04:19,667
flag and a distance within two metres. Contact-
free arrival also excludes any recorded contact
during the episode.

34
00:04:19,667 --> 00:04:27,003
We retain failures, timeouts and contacts. The
full campaign has one thousand three hundred and
eighty-four formal

35
00:04:27,003 --> 00:04:34,338
episodes, with separate denominators for each
diagnostic suite. Independent audits check more
than one point three million

36
00:04:34,338 --> 00:04:41,674
decisions and about sixty-nine thousand model
queries. Uncertainty analysis groups related
scene variants.

37
00:04:43,674 --> 00:04:50,595
Here are all seven natural-scene results on the
same one hundred and twenty conditions. The
geometric selector

38
00:04:50,595 --> 00:04:57,515
records seventy-nine contact-free arrivals,
versus sixty-two for route-constrained speed
forecasting. The primary adapted model reaches
sixty-eight; the

39
00:04:57,515 --> 00:05:04,436
frozen model reaches zero. These results support
the new local planning package in this
simulator. They do

40
00:05:04,436 --> 00:05:11,357
not support a claim that adding a visual model
is the best-performing strategy.

41
00:05:13,357 --> 00:05:19,801
The geometric package improves contact-free
completion by fourteen point two percentage
points over speed forecasting. Its paired

42
00:05:19,801 --> 00:05:26,245
interval is positive, but it is about half a
second slower on ninety-three jointly successful
conditions. The

43
00:05:26,245 --> 00:05:32,690
adapted model is nine point two percentage
points below geometric selection and takes
twelve point two seconds

44
00:05:32,690 --> 00:05:39,134
longer on eighty joint successes. These timing
comparisons exclude failures. All methods share
the new reference route;

45
00:05:39,134 --> 00:05:45,578
the geometric gain concerns the local candidate,
constraint and execution package, while the
learned selector shows no

46
00:05:45,578 --> 00:05:52,022
added benefit in this test.

47
00:05:54,022 --> 00:06:00,483
Changing the training seed gives sixty-eight,
eighteen and fifty-eight contact-free arrivals,
all below the geometric result of

48
00:06:00,483 --> 00:06:06,944
seventy-nine. On twenty-four separate input-
removal conditions, full input gives fourteen
completions, no RGB gives thirteen, no image

49
00:06:06,944 --> 00:06:13,405
gives fifteen, and minimal instruction gives
five. Without an image the model always proposes
the centre path,

50
00:06:13,405 --> 00:06:19,866
while geometric checking remains active. Outside
exposure is higher than with full input. These
findings do not

51
00:06:19,866 --> 00:06:26,327
establish that vision is unnecessary. They show
that task priors, fallback and small correlated
data can dominate

52
00:06:26,327 --> 00:06:32,788
this selector.

53
00:06:34,788 --> 00:06:42,315
The controlled blockage suite places the same
existing object after reference planning.
Geometric selection achieves eleven contact-free

54
00:06:42,315 --> 00:06:49,841
arrivals out of twenty-four; MPPI-style reaches
seven and the adapted model four. On forty curve
conditions, the

55
00:06:49,841 --> 00:06:57,368
model trained without curves reaches three
contact-free arrivals, versus thirty-two after
full-layout training. Removing curves also
reduces

56
00:06:57,368 --> 00:07:04,894
the training sample count, so that difference
mixes layout coverage and data volume.

57
00:07:06,894 --> 00:07:13,791
This intersection demonstration uses the same
fixed illustrative seed for all three
controllers. Each completes without recorded

58
00:07:13,791 --> 00:07:20,687
contact, while their simulated travel times
differ. These three episodes are not part of the
formal test

59
00:07:20,687 --> 00:07:27,584
sample. The demonstration helps explain
behaviour, but it cannot establish an overall
ranking.

60
00:07:41,584 --> 00:07:49,899
In the straight-street example, regulated
tracking arrives but records contact. The
geometric selector arrives without recorded
contact.

61
00:07:49,899 --> 00:07:58,215
The adapted model records no contact but times
out. This separates arrival, contact-free
completion and simply avoiding

62
00:07:58,215 --> 00:08:06,530
contact by waiting. A paused-looking or
stationary robot should not automatically be
described as safe and successful.

63
00:08:20,530 --> 00:08:26,927
The curve example reverses the aggregate
ranking. Regulated tracking and adapted guidance
complete without recorded contact, but

64
00:08:26,927 --> 00:08:33,324
the geometric selector times out and has
substantial outside exposure. A method can lead
on one hundred

65
00:08:33,324 --> 00:08:39,721
and twenty matched conditions and still fail on
a particular scene. This is why we report
footprint

66
00:08:39,721 --> 00:08:46,118
exposure and individual failure cases alongside
overall completion.

67
00:09:00,118 --> 00:09:08,225
Our supported conclusion is that constrained
local planning improves contact-free completion
in these simulated streets. We also

68
00:09:08,225 --> 00:09:16,332
complete real multimodal adapter training, but
its additional navigation benefit is not
established. The project contributes a

69
00:09:16,332 --> 00:09:24,439
transparent comparison across spatial
conditions, rather than a claim of a new
foundation model or state of

70
00:09:24,439 --> 00:09:32,546
the art. Important limits include privileged
observations, a single-frame selector, small
training diversity and approximate dynamics.
Future

71
00:09:32,546 --> 00:09:40,653
temporal observations and better-matched
supervision remain unimplemented. Code, data and
records are public; model weights stay local.

